\section{Selections}{

A selection is sent when the user ends a brush, clicks a mark with
\code{interact(click = "select")}, or clicks a legend entry added with
\code{selection(type = "legend")}. It is not sent while a brush is being
dragged. Clearing a brush (with the "Clear selection" button, Escape, or a
click on an empty part of the plot) sends \code{NULL}, as does clicking a
selected mark again. So does redrawing the plot, which happens when
\code{renderGlyph()} runs again or the output is resized.

For a single plot the value is an integer vector of row numbers, so
\code{data[input$plot_selected, ]} gives the selected rows. Row numbers are
positions in the data frame, not row names. They are correct when rows
with missing values were dropped and when a mapping is computed (such as
\code{x = log(wt)}). Selecting a box, violin, histogram bin or polygon selects
every row it summarises. A mark drawn from its own \code{data} takes no part in
the value. A brush that takes in no rows gives \code{integer(0)}.

A legend selects the rows whose entries are still shown. With every entry
shown there is no selection, and the value is \code{NULL}.

A \code{\link[=facet]{facet()}} grid has one data frame, so its value is one vector: the rows
selected in any of its panels.

A \code{\link[=compose]{compose()}} layout gives a list with one entry per panel, in the order
the panels were given, named after them when \code{compose()} was called with
names. Each entry holds the rows selected in that panel, as row numbers of
that panel's data, or \code{NULL} when nothing is selected there. A panel that
is itself a layout gives a list of the same shape. When no panel has a
selection the whole value is \code{NULL}.

